1
00:00:02,278 --> 00:00:04,188
This is Anti Radar.

2
00:00:04,238 --> 00:00:05,988
It isn't another startup database.

3
00:00:06,038 --> 00:00:09,388
It looks for technical change
before a company becomes obvious.

4
00:00:09,438 --> 00:00:12,176
Everything you'll see is real public data.

5
00:00:13,992 --> 00:00:16,722
This is the morning view for October 20th:

6
00:00:16,772 --> 00:00:19,702
what changed, ranked by evidence.

7
00:00:19,752 --> 00:00:22,622
Three possible formations
are active right now,

8
00:00:22,672 --> 00:00:27,122
five people are worth meeting, and
sixty-four companies are accelerating.

9
00:00:27,172 --> 00:00:31,825
Every row says why it surfaced
now and how many sources back it.

10
00:00:35,139 --> 00:00:39,829
Formation Radar looks for people
and teams before any announcement.

11
00:00:39,879 --> 00:00:43,589
There are six candidates today,
and all of them are early.

12
00:00:43,639 --> 00:00:46,989
The top one is Sameh Khamis, at 44.

13
00:00:47,039 --> 00:00:50,909
His GitHub bio now reads "Founder
and CTO at Stealth Startup",

14
00:00:50,959 --> 00:00:56,209
and it lists NVIDIA, Google and
Microsoft Research before that.

15
00:00:56,259 --> 00:00:58,391
That's self-reported, and
it's labelled that way.

16
00:01:02,193 --> 00:01:04,063
Here the radar holds back.

17
00:01:04,113 --> 00:01:09,003
It's one signal from one source,
GitHub, and no team is linked yet.

18
00:01:09,053 --> 00:01:10,123
Convergence is zero.

19
00:01:10,173 --> 00:01:12,723
So it stays an early signal;

20
00:01:12,773 --> 00:01:14,496
it isn't called a company.

21
00:01:17,182 --> 00:01:20,392
The timeline gives the
dated reason it surfaced:

22
00:01:20,442 --> 00:01:24,372
on October 20th his profile
started saying founder and stealth.

23
00:01:24,422 --> 00:01:29,847
Every item links to its source and says
whether it's a fact or an inference.

24
00:01:36,798 --> 00:01:39,608
The second thread is Kevin Zakka:

25
00:01:39,658 --> 00:01:45,408
272 verified commits to Google
DeepMind's MuJoCo repositories.

26
00:01:45,458 --> 00:01:47,448
In September he started mjbatch,

27
00:01:47,498 --> 00:01:50,448
for running thousands of
physics simulations in parallel,

28
00:01:50,498 --> 00:01:53,948
and it reached 600 stars in 40 days.

29
00:01:53,998 --> 00:01:57,652
That history comes from commit
records, not from his bio.

30
00:02:01,434 --> 00:02:05,844
This is the weekly list, ranked
by evidence rather than fame.

31
00:02:05,894 --> 00:02:08,144
Kevin is number one, at medium priority;

32
00:02:08,194 --> 00:02:10,464
nobody has higher priority this week.

33
00:02:10,514 --> 00:02:13,084
His meeting brief gives thirty-second context,

34
00:02:13,134 --> 00:02:17,844
what he's built, five
questions and what to verify.

35
00:02:17,894 --> 00:02:22,384
Every line is cited and
marked fact or inference.

36
00:02:22,434 --> 00:02:25,728
It's a template, because no
language model is switched on.

37
00:02:34,552 --> 00:02:36,982
ShengShu has the biggest attention gap.

38
00:02:37,032 --> 00:02:41,102
Its new robotics model, Motus2,
gained 616 GitHub stars in 30 days,

39
00:02:41,152 --> 00:02:46,482
and the last week ran almost eight
times faster than the four before it.

40
00:02:46,532 --> 00:02:48,622
Technical momentum is 97;

41
00:02:48,672 --> 00:02:50,462
public attention is 26.

42
00:02:50,512 --> 00:02:52,582
There are no Hacker News threads,

43
00:02:52,632 --> 00:02:57,274
and X and press weren't
measured, so they get no credit.

44
00:03:02,511 --> 00:03:06,141
Investigate turns that
evidence into an Anti Brief:

45
00:03:06,191 --> 00:03:09,701
what they do, why now and what changed.

46
00:03:09,751 --> 00:03:15,321
Where the founders are unknown,
it says so instead of guessing.

47
00:03:15,371 --> 00:03:16,941
Every claim has a source number,

48
00:03:16,991 --> 00:03:21,646
and it warns that single-source
activity is weaker evidence.

49
00:03:24,323 --> 00:03:30,513
Find the technical change early, show
the evidence, and say what we don't know.

50
00:03:30,563 --> 00:03:34,056
Conviction before consensus.
